# The references from one document to another that a filing actually makes. One row per (filing,
# foreign notation) pair it reaches through a part or through the layers an elimination is stated
# between.
#
# This is what an `import` is
#   And `pm.filing_identity` is not it. That table is every document declaring its own URI:
#   `asserted_by` reads "the filing, about itself". In BPMN that is `targetNamespace`, one per
#   document. An `import` is a document naming another one, and nothing in the model files those
#   directly: they are worked out from the references that cross a document boundary.
#
# Parts are not the only reach
#   There are two `pm:ForeignId` references: an `asrt:Part`, and an `asrt:Elimination/between`.
#   Both name a notation and a layer in another document. With no relation calling the second one a
#   reference, every list of what this model reaches would return the parts alone. On the corpus the
#   union changes nothing, because every document a `between` names is already reached by a part.
#   That is luck and not design, and a count cannot tell the difference, which is why the union is
#   taken here.
#
# A count tells an identity from an import
#   An identity table is a `targetNamespace` and not an `import`. A law expecting one import per
#   notation would find the emitter writing fewer, one per document reached, and fewer is right:
#   `asserted_by` reads the filing about itself, where an import is a document naming another. A
#   law is worth having exactly when it can contradict the person who wrote it.
-- diagrams/calls.sqlc's foreign parts, and the filed layers an elimination is stated between.
SELECT DISTINCT x.composition, x.part_notation
FROM (
    SELECT c.composition, c.part_notation
    FROM ( :compose(diagrams/calls.sqlc) ) c
    WHERE NOT c.is_local
    UNION
    SELECT b.composition, b.notation
    FROM ( :compose(eliminations/references.sqlc) ) b
) x
